Root cause:
Lack of privilege check (CAP_NET_ADMIN) when attaching cBPF filter to TCP sockets via SO_ATTACH_FILTER, allowing unprivileged users to potentially leak TCP sequence/acknowledgment numbers.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
case SO_ATTACH_FILTER: {
    struct sock_fprog fprog;
    ret = copy_bpf_fprog_from_user(&fprog, optval, optlen);
    if (!ret)
        ret = sk_attach_filter(&fprog, sk);
    break;
}
```
Reason:  
The `SO_ATTACH_FILTER` case in `sk_setsockopt` performed no privilege check, allowing any unprivileged user to attach a cBPF filter to a TCP socket. This enabled a side-channel attack to leak TCP sequence and acknowledgment numbers.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (sk_is_tcp(sk) &&
    !sockopt_ns_capable(sock_net(sk)->user_ns, CAP_NET_ADMIN)) {
    ret = -EPERM;
    break;
}
```
